From: noname <noname@example.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 20:40:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 14/14] default the interface window's present throttle on

The throttle in 0013 was an opt-in, and the run that never asked for one
died the moment the stream window started presenting:

  opennow-qt: api/gc_egl_wayland.c:2699: buffer_fenced_release:
  Assertion `buffer->release_fence_fd == -1' failed.

weston is 45 ms away from answering a buffer release when the interface
window asks for the next one, and with three images at 60 Hz an image is
back after about 50 ms, so the vendor egl-wayland client rejects the
present. That is the whole crash: the stream window is what forces the
interface window to present again, so the game ends at its first frame
unless the reuse distance already exceeds the lag.

Making two the default costs nothing on the picture: the stream window
draws through its own DMA-BUF surface and keeps its own 60 Hz present
rate, as the same series measured (wayland-video fps=58.6..60, nvst-video
elapsed=730.4s frames=42801). The interface window paints at 30 Hz, which
is the seat UI, not the game. OPENNOW_SWAP_INTERVAL=1 still selects
upstream's every-vsync behaviour for the A/B run, and that is the setting
that reproduces the assertion.

The companion memory change lives on the OS side in
meta-watermelon-wine (transparent_hugepage=madvise in the machine conf):
the kernel report of a running session counted anon_thp:299008kB of the
inactive_anon:491820kB a session holds as huge-page padding, and the
decode then failed its contiguous request
  opennow-linux-v: page allocation failure: order:10, mode:0xcc1(GFP_KERNEL|GFP_DMA)
  cma: cma_alloc: reserved: alloc failed, req-size: 893 pages, ret: -12
which is the same starvation, not a bug in this code.
---

--- a/opennow-qt/src/app/ApplicationStartup.cpp
+++ b/opennow-qt/src/app/ApplicationStartup.cpp
@@ -515,11 +515,19 @@
         // presentation, right at the lag, and the assertion goes off the
         // moment streaming starts to force presents. The swap interval is
         // how many vsyncs must pass before the next presentation, so
-        // OPENNOW_SWAP_INTERVAL=N stretches that distance over the lag; one
-        // is upstream's behaviour and the default. The stream window draws
-        // through its own surface and is not affected.
-        const int swapInterval = qEnvironmentVariableIntValue("OPENNOW_SWAP_INTERVAL");
-        if (swapInterval > 1) {
+        // OPENNOW_SWAP_INTERVAL=N asks for N vsyncs between presentations,
+        // and unset or 0 mean two on this board, because the reuse
+        // distance of a swapchain image has to exceed weston's release lag
+        // before the VeriSilicon egl-wayland client will accept the next
+        // present at all. Two at 60 Hz puts the reuse at about 100 ms
+        // against a 45 ms lag, and the run that had no throttle at all died
+        // with that assertion on the very first presented frame. The stream
+        // window draws through its own surface and is not affected.
+        int swapInterval = qEnvironmentVariableIntValue("OPENNOW_SWAP_INTERVAL");
+        if (swapInterval <= 0) {
+            swapInterval = 2;
+        }
+        if (swapInterval >= 1) {
             auto interfaceFormat = rootWindow->format();
             interfaceFormat.setSwapInterval(swapInterval);
             QSurfaceFormat::setDefaultFormat(interfaceFormat);
